      "spam": false,
      "statusChangeID": null,
      "value": "We went back and forth on this internally, but ultimately tying the head TrackingData to the Selfie Expression head rather than your in-world view camera broke a lot of existing content. Selfie Expression is the first example of the head tracking separately from your point of view and during the beta period for the feature, we did have TrackingData follow the head, but there were many reports of broken worlds (for example world HUDs moving to the side off of your screen if you turn your IRL head). There's still the alternative of using GetBonePosition / Rotation on the head bone which will track with the Selfie Expression head, so I think for now that's our recommendation if something should follow that bone rather than the camera.",
      "viewerReactions": null,
      "voteID": null
    }
  ],
  "companyID": "58c62e995865713647a7115d",
  "created": "2025-04-19T17:38:37.554Z",
  "deletedAt": null,
  "deletedBy": null,
  "details": "(Currently only tested on Desktop)\n\n1. Create/Join a world with an object tracked to your head TrackingData\n\n2. Enable Selfie Cam\n\n3. Look at yourself in a mirror and lean your head left/right\n\n4. Note that the object following your TrackingData does not tilt to match your head\n\nThis doesn’t really make sense to me since the Selfie Cam is indeed “tracking” your head and hands, so Desktop’s TrackingData transforms should be affected by the Selfie Cam too.\n\nAs it stands, this makes it really easy for the Selfie Cam to actually break your immersion in games & worlds that heavily rely on TrackingData.",
